          %h2#literature Literature
          %p
            Related notions:
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hopf+algebroid"} Hopf algebroid
          %h4#commutative_case Commutative case
          %p The commutative case is rather classical. See for example the appendix to
          %ul
            %li
              Douglas C. Ravenel,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Complex cobordism and stable homotopy groups of spheres
              = succeed "." do
                %strong 121
          %h4#noncommutative_case Noncommutative case
          %p The first version of a bialgebroid over a noncommutative base was more narrow:
          %ul
            %li
              M. Sweedler,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Groups of simple algebras
              %a{:href => "http://www.numdam.org/item?id=PMIHES_1974__44__79_0"} numdam
          %p
            A modern generality, but in different early formalism, is due to Takeuchi (who was motivated to generalize the results from the Sweedler’s paper), under the name of
            = succeed "-bialgebra" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo ×
                      %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \times_A
            = succeed "-product," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo ×
                      %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \times_A
            = succeed "):" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Takeuchi+product"} Takeuchi product
          %ul
            %li
              M. Takeuchi,
              %em
                Groups of algebras over
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                        %mo ×
                        %mover
                          %mi A
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \times \bar{A}
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong 29
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.ams.org/mathscinet-getitem?mr=0506407"} MR0506407
                %a{:href => "http://projecteuclid.org/euclid.jmsj/1240432948"} euclid
          %p
            Lu introduces the name bialgebroid for a structure which is equivalent to the Takeuchi’s
            = succeed "-bialgebra" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo ×
                      %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \times_A
          %ul
            %li
              Jiang-Hua Lu,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Hopf algebroids and quantum groupoids
              = succeed "," do
                %strong 7
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/q-alg/9505024"} q-alg/9505024
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://www.ams.org/mathscinet-getitem?mr=95e:16037"} MR95e:16037
              %a{:href => "http://dx.doi.org/10.1142/S0129167X96000050"} doi
          %p Modern treatments are in
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gabriella+B%C3%B6hm"}> Gabriella Böhm
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Internal bialgebroids, entwining structures and corings
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math/0311244"} math.QA/0311244
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong 376
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/G.+B%C3%B6hm"}> G. Böhm
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Hopf algebroids
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0805.3806"} arxiv:math.RA/0805.3806
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Korn%C3%A9l+Szlach%C3%A1nyi"}> Kornél Szlachányi
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The monoidal Eilenberg–Moore construction and bialgebroids
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong 182
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Fiber functors, monoidal sites and Tannaka duality for bialgebroids
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0907.1578"} arxiv/0907.1578
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/T.+Brzezi%C5%84ski"}> T. Brzeziński
                , G. Militaru,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    Bialgebroids,
                    = succeed "-bialgebras" do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %msub
                              %mo ×
                              %mi R
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \times_{R}
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math/0012164"} math.QA/0012164
            %li
              %p
                J. Donin,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andrey+Mudrov"} Andrey Mudrov
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Quantum groupoids and dynamical categories
                %strong 296
                (2006), no. 2, 348–384,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0311316"} math.QA/0311316
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.ams.org/mathscinet-getitem?mr=2201046"} MR2007b:17022
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jalgebra.2006.01.001"} doi
          %p
            There is also a notion of quasibialgebroid, where the coassociativity is weakened by a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bialgebra+cocycle"} bialgebroid 3-cocycle
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hopf+algebroid"} Hopf algebroid
